###############################################################
Key Pandas DataFrame Functions (Methods) & Attributes ->
Here's a breakdown by common data manipulation tasks ->

I. Data Inspection and Overview ->

.head(n=5) -> Returns the first n rows.

.tail(n=5) -> Returns the last n rows.

.info() -> Prints a concise summary of the DataFrame, including data types, non-null values, and memory usage.

.describe() -> Generates descriptive statistics of numerical columns (count, mean, std, min, max, quartiles). Can include non-numeric columns with include='all'.

.shape (attribute) -> Returns a tuple representing the dimensionality (rows, columns).

.size (attribute) -> Returns the total number of elements (rows * columns).

.columns (attribute) -> Returns an Index of the column labels.

.index (attribute) -> Returns the Index (row labels) of the DataFrame.

.dtypes (attribute) -> Returns a Series with the data type of each column.

.empty (attribute) -> Returns True if the DataFrame is empty, False otherwise.

.values (attribute) -> Returns a NumPy representation of the DataFrame's data.

.ndim (attribute) -> Returns the number of array dimensions (always 2 for a DataFrame).

.axes (attribute) -> Returns a list of the row and column axes labels.

II. Selection and Indexing ->

df['column_name'] -> Selects a single column (returns a Series).

df[['col1', 'col2']] -> Selects multiple columns (returns a DataFrame).

.loc[] -> Label-based indexing for selecting rows and columns.

df.loc[row_label, col_label]

df.loc[ ->, 'col_name'] (all rows, specific column)

df.loc[df['col'] > 10, ['col1', 'col2']] (conditional selection)

.iloc[] -> Integer-location based indexing for selecting rows and columns.

df.iloc[row_index, col_index]

df.iloc[0 ->5, 0 ->2] (slicing rows and columns by position)

.at[] -> Fast label-based scalar accessor (for single cell).

.iat[] -> Fast integer-location based scalar accessor (for single cell).

.isin(values) -> Checks whether each element in the DataFrame is contained in values.

.query(expr) -> Filters the DataFrame using a boolean expression (string-based query).

III. Data Cleaning and Handling Missing Data ->

.isnull() / .isna() -> Returns a boolean DataFrame indicating missing (NaN) values.

.notnull() / .notna() -> Returns a boolean DataFrame indicating non-missing values.

.dropna(axis=0, how='any', inplace=False) -> Removes rows or columns with missing values.

axis=0 (rows, default), axis=1 (columns)

how='any' (drop if any NaN), how='all' (drop if all NaN)

inplace=True modifies the DataFrame directly.

.fillna(value, method=None, inplace=False) -> Fills missing values with a specified value or using a method (e.g., 'ffill' for forward fill, 'bfill' for backward fill).

.replace(to_replace, value) -> Replaces specified values in the DataFrame.

IV. Data Manipulation and Transformation ->

.astype(dtype) -> Converts columns to a specified data type.

.rename(mapper=None, index=None, columns=None, inplace=False) -> Renames index or column labels.

.drop(labels, axis=0, inplace=False) -> Drops specified labels from rows or columns.

.drop_duplicates(subset=None, keep='first', inplace=False) -> Removes duplicate rows.

subset -> columns to consider for duplicates.

keep -> 'first', 'last', or False (drop all duplicates).

.apply(func, axis=0) -> Applies a function along an axis of the DataFrame (row-wise or column-wise).

.applymap(func) (Deprecated in modern Pandas for element-wise application; .map() on Series or direct NumPy operations are preferred) -> Applies a function element-wise to the DataFrame.

.assign(**kwargs) -> Assigns new columns to a DataFrame.

.pivot(index, columns, values) -> Reshapes data based on column values.

.pivot_table(values, index, columns, aggfunc='mean') -> Creates a spreadsheet-style pivot table.

.melt(id_vars, value_vars) -> Unpivots a DataFrame from wide to long format.

.stack() -> "Stacks" the columns into rows (creates a MultiIndex Series).

.unstack() -> "Unstacks" a level of the index into columns.

V. Grouping and Aggregation ->

.groupby(by, axis=0) -> Groups DataFrame using a mapper or by a Series of columns.

Common aggregations after groupby() -> .sum(), .mean(), .median(), .min(), .max(), .count(), .size(), .std(), .var(), .agg(), .transform(), .apply().

.agg(func) / .aggregate(func) -> Applies one or more aggregation functions to a DataFrame or grouped object.

.transform(func) -> Performs a group-wise transformation (returns a Series/DataFrame of the same shape as the original).

.value_counts() (Series method, often used on a DataFrame column) -> Returns a Series containing counts of unique values.

VI. Merging, Joining, and Concatenating ->

pd.merge(left, right, on=None, how='inner') -> Combines DataFrames based on common columns or indices (like SQL JOINs).

how -> 'inner', 'outer', 'left', 'right'.

df.join(other, on=None, how='left') -> Combines DataFrames based on index (or a key column) (similar to merge).

pd.concat(objs, axis=0) -> Concatenates Pandas objects along a particular axis (rows or columns).

VII. Sorting ->

.sort_values(by, axis=0, ascending=True, inplace=False) -> Sorts by the values of one or more columns.

.sort_index(axis=0, level=None, ascending=True, inplace=False) -> Sorts by index labels.

VIII. Mathematical and Statistical Operations ->

.sum(axis=0) -> Sum of values.

.mean(axis=0) -> Mean of values.

.median(axis=0) -> Median of values.

.min(axis=0) -> Minimum value.

.max(axis=0) -> Maximum value.

.std(axis=0) -> Standard deviation.

.corr() -> Computes pairwise correlation of columns.

.cov() -> Computes pairwise covariance of columns.

.rank(axis=0, method='average') -> Computes numerical data ranks.

.diff(periods=1, axis=0) -> First discrete difference of element.

.pct_change(periods=1) -> Percentage change.

.clip(lower, upper) -> Trim values at input thresholds.

.round(decimals) -> Rounds to a variable number of decimal places.

.abs() -> Absolute numerical value of each element.

IX. Time Series Functionality ->

pd.to_datetime() -> Converts argument to datetime objects.

.resample(rule) -> Resamples time-series data.

.asfreq(freq) -> Converts time series to specified frequency.

X. Input/Output (often pd.read_... and df.to_...)

pd.read_csv(filepath_or_buffer) -> Reads a CSV file into a DataFrame.

pd.read_excel(io) -> Reads an Excel file into a DataFrame.

pd.read_sql(sql_query, con) -> Reads SQL query or database table into a DataFrame.

df.to_csv(path_or_buf) -> Writes DataFrame to a CSV file.

df.to_excel(excel_writer) -> Writes DataFrame to an Excel file.

df.to_json(path_or_buf) -> Writes DataFrame to a JSON file.

df.to_sql(name, con) -> Writes DataFrame to a SQL table.

XI. Plotting ->

.plot() -> Makes plots of DataFrame columns. This is a wrapper around Matplotlib.

df.plot.line(), df.plot.bar(), df.plot.hist(), df.plot.scatter(), etc.
